1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,680
At first, lightweight white stones began
falling on Pompeii.

2
00:00:05,140 --> 00:00:09,840
People sheltered beneath their roofs and
shut their doors, hoping to wait out the

3
00:00:09,840 --> 00:00:13,200
strange storm. But the stones kept coming.

4
00:00:13,920 --> 00:00:16,240
Roofs began to collapse under their
weight.

5
00:00:16,560 --> 00:00:21,520
And by the following morning, the city
would face a danger that neither doors nor

6
00:00:21,520 --> 00:00:23,000
walls could keep out.

7
00:00:23,860 --> 00:00:26,720
It happened in the year 79 AD.

8
00:00:27,440 --> 00:00:32,820
To understand how one of the most famous
cities of ancient Rome was destroyed, we

9
00:00:32,820 --> 00:00:38,220
need to reconstruct not only the eruption
of Mount Vesuvius, but also the decisions

10
00:00:38,220 --> 00:00:39,780
its residents had to make.

11
00:00:40,180 --> 00:00:41,880
Should they leave or stay?

12
00:00:42,500 --> 00:00:46,460
Run through streets where stones were
falling from the sky, or take

13
00:00:46,460 --> 00:00:48,580
shelter in a building that might collapse?

14
00:00:49,500 --> 00:00:54,460
We don't know what happened to every
family, and we won't invent anyone's final

15
00:00:54,460 --> 00:01:00,140
conversations. But we do have letters
written by an eyewitness, marks

16
00:01:00,140 --> 00:01:05,880
left on walls, objects found inside homes,
and layers of volcanic

17
00:01:05,880 --> 00:01:11,840
rock. Together, they let us piece together
the disaster, almost hour by

18
00:01:11,840 --> 00:01:17,740
hour. On an ordinary day, Pompeii was a
bustling trading city on the

19
00:01:17,740 --> 00:01:21,140
Bay of Naples. Carts rolled through its
streets.

20
00:01:21,640 --> 00:01:27,640
Workshops made goods, bakeries ground
grain and baked bread, and stalls sold

21
00:01:27,640 --> 00:01:28,800
ready-to-eat food.

22
00:01:29,340 --> 00:01:34,560
People covered walls with announcements,
campaign slogans, and personal messages.

23
00:01:35,740 --> 00:01:40,960
Some residents lived in spacious houses
with painted walls and interior gardens.

24
00:01:41,580 --> 00:01:46,280
Others worked in shops, rented modest
rooms, or lived in slavery.

25
00:01:46,280 --> 00:01:49,640
Pompeii wasn't just a city of wealthy
Romans.

26
00:01:50,180 --> 00:01:54,420
Its people had very different rights,
resources, and opportunities.

27
00:01:55,320 --> 00:01:58,580
Rising above the rooftops was Mount
Vesuvius.

28
00:01:59,080 --> 00:02:01,600
Fields and vineyards covered its slopes.

29
00:02:02,240 --> 00:02:07,780
Today we know it was a volcano, but to
most people living nearby, it did not look

30
00:02:07,780 --> 00:02:09,920
like a warning of imminent destruction.

31
00:02:10,520 --> 00:02:16,080
Vesuvius had gone a very long time without
a major eruption, and memories of earlier

32
00:02:16,080 --> 00:02:20,000
disasters offered little clear
understanding of what was coming.

33
00:02:21,040 --> 00:02:24,620
The ground in this region, however, was
far from peaceful.

34
00:02:25,280 --> 00:02:30,080
Seventeen years before Pompeii's
destruction, a powerful earthquake had

35
00:02:30,080 --> 00:02:31,800
damaged buildings and streets.

36
00:02:32,380 --> 00:02:37,040
The city was still being rebuilt, and
archaeologists can see traces of those

37
00:02:37,040 --> 00:02:42,560
repairs today. More earthquakes followed,
but an earthquake alone did not tell

38
00:02:42,560 --> 00:02:46,480
residents that an eruption of this
magnitude was about to happen.

39
00:02:47,240 --> 00:02:52,340
Sometime in late summer or fall of 79 AD,
an ordinary

40
00:02:52,340 --> 00:02:53,520
day changed.

41
00:02:54,380 --> 00:02:56,860
Scholars still debate the exact date.

42
00:02:57,520 --> 00:03:02,060
August twenty-fourth was long considered
the traditional date, but archaeological

43
00:03:02,060 --> 00:03:04,560
evidence also points toward the fall.

44
00:03:05,240 --> 00:03:09,120
For this story, the sequence of events
matters more than the season.

45
00:03:09,900 --> 00:03:15,200
Sometime after noon, an enormous cloud
rose from Mount Vesuvius.

46
00:03:15,760 --> 00:03:19,980
It shot upward, then spread into wide
branches near the top.

47
00:03:20,540 --> 00:03:25,380
One of the main eyewitnesses, Pliny the
Younger, compared its shape to a pine

48
00:03:25,380 --> 00:03:29,560
tree. He was watching from Misenum, on the
other side of the bay.

49
00:03:29,880 --> 00:03:31,380
Not from Pompeii itself.

50
00:03:32,360 --> 00:03:36,380
What Pliny described was not ordinary
smoke from a fire.

51
00:03:37,100 --> 00:03:43,100
Magma was rising inside the volcano, and
gases were forcing huge quantities of

52
00:03:43,100 --> 00:03:46,440
scorching fragments and ash into the
atmosphere.

53
00:03:47,100 --> 00:03:51,480
A towering eruption column rose tens of
kilometers into the sky.

54
00:03:52,080 --> 00:03:56,340
The wind carried its contents toward the
towns south of Vesuvius.

55
00:03:57,220 --> 00:04:01,160
That wind determined which danger would
reach Pompeii first.

56
00:04:01,640 --> 00:04:07,320
The city began to be covered in pumice: a
light, porous rock formed from frothy

57
00:04:07,320 --> 00:04:08,580
volcanic material.

58
00:04:09,120 --> 00:04:13,520
One piece can look almost harmless,
especially compared with glowing lava.

59
00:04:14,200 --> 00:04:20,060
But imagine not one little stone, but a
continuous rain of rock that goes on for

60
00:04:20,060 --> 00:04:25,700
hours. Stones cover roads and courtyards,
piling up on roofs and at

61
00:04:25,700 --> 00:04:29,720
doorways. Each new layer makes the streets
harder to cross.

62
00:04:29,720 --> 00:04:34,720
What you might brush off your shoulder at
first gradually becomes a serious

63
00:04:34,720 --> 00:04:40,040
obstruction. For someone inside a house,
the temptation is obvious:

64
00:04:40,620 --> 00:04:46,080
wait it out. A roof offers some protection
from falling debris, and outside

65
00:04:46,080 --> 00:04:48,240
it's difficult to see where you're going.

66
00:04:48,740 --> 00:04:51,400
But pumice keeps accumulating overhead.

67
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:55,660
Eventually, its weight becomes dangerous
for the roof structure.

68
00:04:55,660 --> 00:05:01,580
In Pompeii, archaeologists have found the
remains of people killed when roofs and

69
00:05:01,580 --> 00:05:02,640
walls collapsed.

70
00:05:03,400 --> 00:05:07,160
Some residents did leave while an escape
route was still open.

71
00:05:07,700 --> 00:05:11,880
That's an important part of the story, and
one that movies often overlook.

72
00:05:12,400 --> 00:05:17,200
Pompeii was not a city where everyone
stayed and died at the same moment.

73
00:05:17,640 --> 00:05:22,560
We don't know exactly how many people
escaped, but evidence survives of people

74
00:05:22,560 --> 00:05:27,060
who lived through the eruption and of
later attempts to return for possessions.

75
00:05:27,980 --> 00:05:33,000
Meanwhile, on the other side of the bay,
another dramatic event was unfolding.

76
00:05:33,700 --> 00:05:38,900
Pliny the Elder, the eyewitness's uncle,
commanded the Roman fleet at Misenum.

77
00:05:39,420 --> 00:05:43,840
At first, the unusual cloud interested him
as a subject of study.

78
00:05:44,360 --> 00:05:47,040
He planned to sail closer and investigate
it.

79
00:05:47,440 --> 00:05:51,560
Then a woman living near the volcano sent
a request for help.

80
00:05:52,080 --> 00:05:53,280
His mission changed.

81
00:05:54,040 --> 00:05:58,920
Pliny ordered ships to put to sea and
headed toward a place others were trying

82
00:05:58,920 --> 00:06:04,520
to escape. The closer the vessels came to
shore, the thicker the falling ash

83
00:06:04,520 --> 00:06:07,480
became, and the more debris struck the
water.

84
00:06:08,080 --> 00:06:10,480
They couldn't reach their intended
destination.

85
00:06:11,600 --> 00:06:15,420
Eventually, Pliny arrived at Stabiae,
where his acquaintance

86
00:06:15,540 --> 00:06:16,980
Pomponianus was staying.

87
00:06:17,960 --> 00:06:22,020
This was not a rescue directly from the
city gates of Pompeii.

88
00:06:22,440 --> 00:06:25,180
The letter describes another part of the
coastline.

89
00:06:25,640 --> 00:06:30,920
But it reveals the larger situation:
people were trying to escape by land and

90
00:06:30,920 --> 00:06:35,100
by sea, while the eruption was steadily
cutting off possible routes.

91
00:06:35,820 --> 00:06:39,760
By evening, Pompeii was far darker than
usual.

92
00:06:40,280 --> 00:06:43,880
Almost no sunlight could penetrate the
volcanic cloud.

93
00:06:44,340 --> 00:06:47,840
The streets were buried beneath mountains
of pumice.

94
00:06:48,320 --> 00:06:51,860
Familiar routes through the city had
become difficult to navigate.

95
00:06:52,380 --> 00:06:56,920
With every step, it grew harder to avoid
stumbling over stones.

96
00:06:57,940 --> 00:07:00,640
And there was another danger: earthquakes.

97
00:07:01,560 --> 00:07:05,280
As the eruption continued, the shaking
intensified.

98
00:07:05,940 --> 00:07:10,880
Buildings already weakened by earlier
damage and the weight of fallen material

99
00:07:10,880 --> 00:07:16,820
could collapse. A study published in 2024
suggests that some residents

100
00:07:16,820 --> 00:07:20,420
may have died because of
earthquake-related building collapses

101
00:07:20,420 --> 00:07:25,600
during the eruption, rather than directly
from volcanic ash or extreme heat.

102
00:07:25,980 --> 00:07:28,220
It was a terrifying choice.

103
00:07:28,780 --> 00:07:31,540
Outside, stones were falling from the sky.

104
00:07:32,080 --> 00:07:34,300
Inside, the ceiling could come down.

105
00:07:34,640 --> 00:07:39,500
And no one could know how long it would
last: another hour, or another week.

106
00:07:39,960 --> 00:07:44,460
There were no modern alerts, no
communication networks, and no maps

107
00:07:44,460 --> 00:07:45,560
showing safe zones.

108
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:50,300
People had to make decisions based on what
they could see and hear around them.

109
00:07:51,080 --> 00:07:56,060
Pliny the Younger recorded that, across
the bay, people called out to locate their

110
00:07:56,060 --> 00:07:59,840
relatives because darkness had swallowed
everything around them.

111
00:08:00,200 --> 00:08:02,380
He himself left town with his mother.

112
00:08:02,800 --> 00:08:07,980
According to his account, the ground shook
so violently that carts would not stay in

113
00:08:07,980 --> 00:08:11,080
place, even with stones wedged beneath
their wheels.

114
00:08:11,920 --> 00:08:16,820
While pumice continued to rain down on
Pompeii, the neighboring city of

115
00:08:16,820 --> 00:08:19,940
Herculaneum faced a different sequence of
events.

116
00:08:20,700 --> 00:08:25,040
At first, the wind carried most of the
falling stones away from it.

117
00:08:25,260 --> 00:08:27,440
But that did not mean it was safe.

118
00:08:28,140 --> 00:08:34,060
During the night, pyroclastic flows rushed
down the slopes of Vesuvius: mixtures of

119
00:08:34,060 --> 00:08:39,840
hot gas, ash, and rock fragments moving at
tremendous speed close to the ground.

120
00:08:40,720 --> 00:08:45,880
These flows are nothing like a slow river
of lava that you might have time to walk

121
00:08:45,880 --> 00:08:51,780
around. They spread rapidly, pour into
low-lying areas, and destroy what lies

122
00:08:51,780 --> 00:08:56,660
in their path. Herculaneum was struck by
this danger before Pompeii.

123
00:08:57,040 --> 00:09:00,620
Its buildings and streets were buried
beneath a thick layer of

124
00:09:00,620 --> 00:09:01,720
volcanic deposits.

125
00:09:02,660 --> 00:09:06,780
Pompeii, meanwhile, was still enduring the
rain of stones.

126
00:09:07,480 --> 00:09:09,460
It continued for many hours.

127
00:09:10,080 --> 00:09:14,860
By the following morning, around three
meters of pumice had accumulated in some

128
00:09:14,860 --> 00:09:20,060
places. Picture the height of the ceiling
in an ordinary room, and imagine almost

129
00:09:20,060 --> 00:09:23,100
that much loose rock piled up in the
streets.

130
00:09:23,680 --> 00:09:28,160
Opening a door, climbing out of a
ground-floor room, or crossing a mound of

131
00:09:28,160 --> 00:09:31,000
debris had become extraordinarily
difficult.

132
00:09:31,760 --> 00:09:34,800
Then the intensity of the falling stones
changed.

133
00:09:35,360 --> 00:09:38,440
For a short time, it may have seemed that
the worst was over.

134
00:09:38,880 --> 00:09:44,000
But the layers of volcanic deposits tell
scientists this was not the end of the

135
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:47,420
eruption. Conditions above Vesuvius were
changing.

136
00:09:47,940 --> 00:09:52,640
Parts of the enormous eruption column
collapsed, sending new pyroclastic flows

137
00:09:52,640 --> 00:09:54,240
racing across the ground.

138
00:09:54,880 --> 00:09:57,840
One of them reached Pompeii the following
morning.

139
00:09:58,400 --> 00:10:03,900
A powerful mixture of volcanic gases, fine
ash, and hot rock fragments

140
00:10:03,900 --> 00:10:07,320
swept into places where people had tried
to hide.

141
00:10:07,780 --> 00:10:13,040
It moved far faster than a human could
run, and it didn't need an open door to

142
00:10:13,040 --> 00:10:15,480
turn a house into a deadly trap.

143
00:10:16,330 --> 00:10:21,280
This is where the familiar movie image of
Pompeii's destruction falls apart.

144
00:10:21,660 --> 00:10:25,880
The city was not simply swallowed by a
flood of glowing red lava.

145
00:10:26,380 --> 00:10:31,340
The main destructive forces were falling
pumice, collapsing buildings, earthquakes,

146
00:10:31,920 --> 00:10:33,400
and pyroclastic flows.

147
00:10:33,960 --> 00:10:38,660
Different people died in different
circumstances, and scientists are still

148
00:10:38,660 --> 00:10:41,360
working to understand the precise
mechanisms.

149
00:10:42,200 --> 00:10:47,420
One study estimated that a deadly flow
reaching Pompeii may have affected the

150
00:10:47,420 --> 00:10:50,000
city for around ten to twenty minutes.

151
00:10:50,600 --> 00:10:55,600
For people trapped in a cloud filled with
ash they could inhale, that was far too

152
00:10:55,600 --> 00:11:01,280
long. Other studies and museum accounts
emphasize the effects of intense heat.

153
00:11:01,760 --> 00:11:06,660
We cannot determine the exact cause of
death of every person whose remains have

154
00:11:06,660 --> 00:11:12,220
been found. What is clear is that once
these flows arrived, those still

155
00:11:12,220 --> 00:11:15,340
inside the city had almost no chance of
escaping.

156
00:11:15,960 --> 00:11:21,500
Places where homes, streets, workshops,
and gardens had stood only hours earlier

157
00:11:21,500 --> 00:11:23,720
were covered by volcanic deposits.

158
00:11:24,300 --> 00:11:27,260
The ancient city we know today was buried.

159
00:11:28,040 --> 00:11:31,160
Pliny the Elder never returned from his
voyage.

160
00:11:31,720 --> 00:11:33,140
He died at Stabiae.

161
00:11:33,720 --> 00:11:39,280
Years later, his nephew described both
that journey and his own escape in letters

162
00:11:39,280 --> 00:11:41,040
to the historian Tacitus.

163
00:11:41,620 --> 00:11:46,780
We know an extraordinary amount about this
disaster because that human eyewitness

164
00:11:46,780 --> 00:11:51,440
account survived. But the letters were
written after the eruption, so their

165
00:11:51,440 --> 00:11:54,640
details have to be compared with the
archaeological evidence.

166
00:11:55,460 --> 00:11:59,140
Life in the region did not end forever
after the disaster.

167
00:11:59,680 --> 00:12:01,020
Some residents survived.

168
00:12:01,700 --> 00:12:05,440
Others tried to return and recover
whatever belongings were left.

169
00:12:05,960 --> 00:12:08,860
Officials under Emperor Titus dealt with
the aftermath.

170
00:12:09,540 --> 00:12:13,580
People continued searching the site of
Pompeii for building materials and

171
00:12:13,580 --> 00:12:18,280
possessions. But the city as it had once
functioned was never restored.

172
00:12:19,180 --> 00:12:24,160
Over time, soil and new layers of human
activity covered the ruins.

173
00:12:24,760 --> 00:12:27,280
The memory of the city gradually faded.

174
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:31,720
Systematic excavations began here in the
eighteenth century.

175
00:12:32,260 --> 00:12:37,360
Researchers went on to discover not only
walls and household objects, but also

176
00:12:37,360 --> 00:12:40,300
traces of the people caught in the
catastrophe.

177
00:12:41,080 --> 00:12:45,760
One of Pompeii's most famous features is
the plaster casts of the victims.

178
00:12:46,620 --> 00:12:51,420
These are not people who turned to stone,
as photographs sometimes make them appear.

179
00:12:51,960 --> 00:12:57,500
Their bodies decomposed over time, leaving
hollow spaces in the hardened volcanic

180
00:12:57,500 --> 00:13:03,220
material. In the nineteenth century,
archaeologist Giuseppe Fiorelli developed

181
00:13:03,220 --> 00:13:08,440
a method of pouring plaster into those
spaces, recreating the shapes of bodies

182
00:13:08,440 --> 00:13:10,480
and even the folds of clothing.

183
00:13:11,440 --> 00:13:15,100
The remains of more than a thousand
victims have been found.

184
00:13:15,500 --> 00:13:20,820
But that is not a complete death toll, and
certainly not a count of Pompeii's entire

185
00:13:20,820 --> 00:13:26,280
population. Archaeologists have not
investigated every location, and many

186
00:13:26,280 --> 00:13:27,980
people managed to leave the city.

187
00:13:28,520 --> 00:13:33,860
What survives tells us about individual
human lives, but it cannot give us a

188
00:13:33,860 --> 00:13:38,220
complete record of every resident and
every final decision they made.

189
00:13:38,960 --> 00:13:42,360
And that is what makes Pompeii so
extraordinary.

190
00:13:42,980 --> 00:13:48,280
Almost two thousand years separate us from
its inhabitants, yet we can still see a

191
00:13:48,280 --> 00:13:53,320
street, a bakery, a wall covered in
writing, and dishes used for meals.

192
00:13:53,740 --> 00:13:58,400
We can glimpse a world where people made
plans for tomorrow, repaired their homes,

193
00:13:58,780 --> 00:14:00,040
worked, and argued.

194
00:14:00,500 --> 00:14:05,300
They had no idea that their ordinary day
would become a chapter in history.

195
00:14:06,060 --> 00:14:11,940
Today, almost everyone knows Pompeii as
the city destroyed by Mount Vesuvius.

196
00:14:12,400 --> 00:14:17,900
But to the people living there in 79 AD,
it was, above all, home.

197
00:14:18,580 --> 00:14:23,200
And when that enormous cloud rose above
the mountain, they were not facing an

198
00:14:23,200 --> 00:14:26,680
abstract historical question about how
volcanoes work.

199
00:14:27,160 --> 00:14:32,320
They faced one immediate, practical
question: would they make it out alive?

200
00:14:33,240 --> 00:14:38,820
Some did. Others remained beneath roofs
that could no longer bear the weight of

201
00:14:38,820 --> 00:14:44,600
the stones. Still others survived the long
night, only to be trapped when the

202
00:14:44,600 --> 00:14:46,280
scorching cloud arrived.

203
00:14:47,120 --> 00:14:52,360
The ash buried the city, but it preserved
traces of the lives lived there.

204
00:14:52,900 --> 00:14:58,620
And today, whenever archaeologists uncover
another street or room, we learn not

205
00:14:58,620 --> 00:15:00,880
only about Pompeii's final day.

206
00:15:01,260 --> 00:15:05,640
We learn about the thousands of ordinary
days that came before it.

